Because training agents only on isolated short tasks (the “modular” approach) makes each agent competent but leaves a gap in how agents should hand off information—so errors still propagate down the pipeline. The paper therefore constructs a long‑trajectory subset that explicitly mimics inference-time execution so the model can learn those handoffs and inter-agent logic under realistic conditions.

Concretely, the long‑trajectory data:
- reproduces the full pipeline (intent → retrieval → fact location → generation), including retrieved documents and critic‑labeled relevances and fact spans, so downstream agents see the same noisy/structured inputs they will encounter at inference;  
- uses trajectory head/end tokens and assembles full agent trajectories so training supervises not only each agent’s outputs but also the transition from one agent’s end token e^T to the next agent’s head token h^{T+1};  
- thus teaches inter‑agent coordination (how outputs should be formatted, interpreted, corrected, or ignored) rather than only per‑agent competence.

In short, the long‑trajectory subset is needed to close the “gap between modules”: it lets long‑trajectory learning teach robust handoffs and error‑handling across agents (reducing cumulative error), while short‑trajectory data builds strong per‑agent skills. This combination yields better synergy, interpretability, and robustness at inference.
